          "kind": "+",
          "text": "  <main class=\"manual-content\">",
          "old": null,
          "new": 32
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    <h1>Robot manual</h1>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 33
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    <p class=\"lede\">A field guide to writing programs that fight on a virtual battlefield.</p>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 34
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    <section id=\"intro\"><h2>What crystal-robots is</h2>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 35
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "      <p>crystal-robots is a Crystal-flavored take on Tom Poindexter’s <a href=\"http://tpoindex.github.io/crobots/\">CROBOTS</a> (1985). Its WebAssembly compiler grows out of Colin Eberhardt’s <a href=\"https://github.com/ColinEberhardt/chasm\">chasm</a>. Here, the compiler and the battle run entirely in your browser.</p>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 36
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "      <p>You write a small robot program, test it against others, and watch the match. The language is a focused subset, so use the forms described below rather than assuming all of Crystal is available.</p>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 37
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    </section>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 38
        }
      ],
      "lines": [
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    <section id=\"first\"><h2>Your first robot</h2>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 39
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "      <p>Paste this complete program into the <a href=\"../editor/\">robot editor</a> and choose Fight:</p>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 40
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "<pre><code class=\"language-crystal-robots\">main(\"Scout\") do",
          "old": null,
          "new": 41
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "  drive(0, 50)",
          "old": null,
          "new": 42
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "  while true",
          "old": null,
          "new": 43
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    range = scan(0, 10)",
          "old": null,
          "new": 44
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    if range > 0",
          "old": null,
          "new": 45
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "      cannon(0, range)",
          "old": null,
          "new": 46
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    end",
          "old": null,
          "new": 47
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    sleep",
          "old": null,
          "new": 48
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "  end",
          "old": null,
          "new": 49
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "end</code></pre>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 50
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "      <p><code>main(\"Scout\") do</code> names your robot and opens its program. <code>drive(0, 50)</code> asks it to head east at speed 50. <code>while true</code> repeats the body. <code>scan(0, 10)</code> looks east within ten degrees; it returns the closest robot’s distance, or zero. When the distance is positive, <code>cannon(0, range)</code> fires toward it. <code>sleep</code> spends a turn while motion continues. Each <code>end</code> closes its matching block. Try another angle or speed and watch the replay.</p>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 51
        }
      ],
      "after": [
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    </section>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 52
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "    <section id=\"language\"><h2>The language</h2>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 53
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "      <p>Programs use integer expressions and line endings or semicolons between statements. Comments start with <code>#</code> and run to the end of a line. The only string literal used by this subset is the robot name in <code>main(\"Name\") do … end</code>. A top-level <code>def</code> declares a function; <code>return</code> may give back a value. <code>global(name, init)</code> declares mutable shared state; a top-level assignment also makes an implicit global constant. Function definitions are collected before checking calls, so source order does not matter.</p>",
          "old": null,
          "new": 54
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "<pre><code class=\"language-crystal-robots\"># Keep a shared shot count.",
          "old": null,
          "new": 55
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "global(shots, 0)",
          "old": null,
          "new": 56
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "LIMIT = 3",
          "old": null,
          "new": 57
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "",
          "old": null,
          "new": 58
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "def double(value)",
          "old": null,
          "new": 59
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "  return value * 2",
          "old": null,
          "new": 60
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "end",
          "old": null,
          "new": 61
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "",
          "old": null,
          "new": 62
        },
        {
          "kind": "+",
          "text": "main(\"Counter\") do",
          "old": null,
          "new": 63
        }
      ],
